dged-text identity), and its added evidence nodes exist and passed at 013.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=2\n\n## first-approval\n\nFour new rows await a first approval under SR-174. The stopping boundary recorded at 013 holds:\n- **LLR-362** is allocation, the record's writer and range rule, verified by TC-401 with TC-158.\n- **LLR-363** is admission, what a landing accepts against the record, verified by TC-402.\n- The work-item half stays with LLR-153 and LLR-154.\n\nSplitting further, by argument case or by landing route, would restate one decision over different inputs.\n\n- [APPROVE] LLR-362 -> The carrier is a shared ref, apart from any lane's branch, holding the watermark for the lane-minted spaces. A reservation moves it by compare-and-swap and appends one record per space. Each range starts one above the larger of the carrier's and the integration branch's committed mark, so ranges are disjoint from any bases. A refused argument or a lost swap writes nothing. The first reservation creates the carrier from the integration branch's committed watermark. Only a reservation writes a record, and only a fold copies one. A fold yields maximum marks, the record unions and the receiving tree's basis. Committed records stay unchanged across every parent, writers keep them, and a mark rises only by allocation, ruled correction or a covering record -> its text is unchanged since sitting 014 approved it, and only its traced symbols gained back-links. It matches the code (`plan_reservation`, `reserve`, `fold`) -> ready. 014's note stands: the fold sentence does not say the carrier contributes only its records and the marks they reserve, and the row's own rise rule bounds that.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-363 -> as approved at 013, plus one refusal. Independently of the added identities, an identity live in the landing tree that a retirement record the landing brings names (held by the landing tree and not by its parent) is refused, naming the identity and the record. A retirement record and live identity both already held by the parent do not refuse -> it is sitting 018's FIX, stated in its own narrow form. The code checks it first, before the added-identity rules and before a no-addition landing passes (`landing_lines` with `_reissued_ids`), on both routes. TC-402 drives it on both callers, and the legacy pair is driven as passing -> ready:\n  - It closes both re-issue shapes, in range and base-live, without blocking landings over a pair trunk already holds.\n  - It names only the watermark.\n  - Note, not a finding: \"Independently of added identities, before a landing that adds none passes, ...\" is clumsy. It means that the refusal applies to every landing, a landing that adds nothing included. Its first clause and the code both read that way, so it does not restrict the rule to landings that add nothing.\n- [APPROVE] TC-401 -> carrier unit and repository cases: zero-write refusals, disjoint sequential and stale-base ranges from the applicable mark, a lost swap writing nothing, first-use seeding, and folds keeping maximum marks and every valid record once -> its text is unchanged since sitting 014 approved it, and its evidence nodes exist -> ready.\n- [APPROVE] TC-402 -> real merge-slot and hand-squash landing cases: every landing-table outcome; a reserved identity authored, retired and re-authored; an identity live at the lane's base, retired and re-authored in the lane, refused at both landings naming its record; a lane beside a pair trunk already held, which lands; each route carrying the carrier fold; and a watermark-only refresh conflict -> it is closed over LLR-363's arms, the new refusal and its legacy exemption included. The four retirement cases run here pass -> ready.\n\n**Parent-first.** SR-174's amendment is blessed in this same sitting, and all four children are approved together.\n\n**What the act needs.** The rung is released, so the later act turn does both parts in one act:\n- re-attest SR-174 and TC-158;\n- flip LLR-362, LLR-3